solutions.OOPJ-B4-1762:
### Multithreaded Java Program with Synchronized Methods

**Problem without synchronization:** Multiple threads accessing shared data simultaneously causes **race conditions** (incorrect results).

**Solution:** Use `synchronized` to ensure only **one thread at a time** accesses the critical section.

#### Example – Thread-Safe Bank Account:
```java
class BankAccount {
    private double balance;

    BankAccount(double initialBalance) {
        this.balance = initialBalance;
    }

    // Synchronized method – only one thread can execute at a time
    public synchronized void deposit(double amount) {
        balance += amount;
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                + " deposited " + amount + " | Balance: " + balance);
    }

    public synchronized void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (balance >= amount) {
            balance -= amount;
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                    + " withdrew " + amount + " | Balance: " + balance);
        } else {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                    + " – Insufficient funds");
        }
    }

    public synchronized double getBalance() { return balance; }
}

class BankTask extends Thread {
    BankAccount account;
    String action;
    double amount;

    BankTask(BankAccount account, String action, double amount, String name) {
        super(name);
        this.account = account;
        this.action = action;
        this.amount = amount;
    }

    public void run() {
        if (action.equals("deposit"))
            account.deposit(amount);
        else
            account.withdraw(amount);
    }
}

public class SynchronizationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        BankAccount account = new BankAccount(1000);

        Thread t1 = new BankTask(account, "deposit",  500, "Thread-Deposit");
        Thread t2 = new BankTask(account, "withdraw", 300, "Thread-Withdraw1");
        Thread t3 = new BankTask(account, "withdraw", 800, "Thread-Withdraw2");

        t1.start(); t2.start(); t3.start();
        t1.join(); t2.join(); t3.join();

        System.out.println("Final Balance: " + account.getBalance());
    }
}
```

**Output (order may vary, but balance is always correct):**
```
Thread-Deposit deposited 500.0 | Balance: 1500.0
Thread-Withdraw1 withdrew 300.0 | Balance: 1200.0
Thread-Withdraw2 withdrew 800.0 | Balance: 400.0
Final Balance: 400.0
```

