### Thread-Based vs Process-Based Multitasking

| Feature | Process-Based Multitasking | Thread-Based Multitasking |
|---------|---------------------------|--------------------------|
| **Unit** | Process (entire program) | Thread (lightweight unit within process) |
| **Memory** | Each process has its own memory space | Threads share the same memory space (heap) |
| **Communication** | Complex (IPC – pipes, sockets, shared memory) | Easy – threads can share variables directly |
| **Overhead** | High – process creation is expensive | Low – thread creation is fast and cheap |
| **Context switch** | Slow – full memory space switch | Fast – only registers and stack change |
| **Isolation** | High – crash of one process doesn't affect another | Low – crash of one thread can affect all |
| **Example** | Running Notepad + Chrome + Java program simultaneously | Multiple threads within a single Java program |
| **OS resource** | Each process has own file handles, registers | Threads share file handles within a process |

**Thread-Based Example in Java:**
```java
class Task extends Thread {
    String name;
    Task(String name) { this.name = name; }
    public void run() {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++)
            System.out.println(name + " – step " + i);
    }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Task("Thread-A").start();
        new Task("Thread-B").start();
        // Both run concurrently, sharing the same process memory
    }
}
```

**Conclusion:** Thread-based multitasking is more **efficient** than process-based for tasks that need to share data, since threads share memory and are lighter weight than processes.

