### Constructor Overloading in Java

**Definition:** Constructor overloading means defining **multiple constructors** in the same class, each with a **different number or type of parameters**.

**Purpose:** Allows creating objects in different ways depending on what data is available.

**Example:**
```java
class Rectangle {
    int length, width;

    // Constructor 1 – no args (default dimensions)
    Rectangle() {
        length = 1;
        width = 1;
    }

    // Constructor 2 – one arg (square)
    Rectangle(int side) {
        length = side;
        width = side;
    }

    // Constructor 3 – two args
    Rectangle(int length, int width) {
        this.length = length;
        this.width = width;
    }

    int area() { return length * width; }
}

// Creating objects using different constructors:
Rectangle r1 = new Rectangle();       // uses Constructor 1
Rectangle r2 = new Rectangle(5);      // uses Constructor 2
Rectangle r3 = new Rectangle(4, 6);   // uses Constructor 3
```

**Key point:** Java determines which constructor to call based on the **number and type of arguments** (this is compile-time/static binding).

