### Differentiate Between Compile-Time and Runtime Polymorphism in Java

Polymorphism allows one interface to be used for a general class of actions. In Java, it is implemented in two distinct forms:

| Feature | Compile-Time Polymorphism | Runtime Polymorphism |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Alternative Names** | Static Polymorphism / Early Binding | Dynamic Polymorphism / Late Binding |
| **Mechanism** | Method Overloading (and Operator Overloading) | Method Overriding via Dynamic Method Dispatch |
| **Execution Point** | Resolved by compiler during compilation | Resolved by JVM at runtime based on object type |
| **Inheritance Requirement** | Not required (happens in the same class) | Mandatory (occurs between superclass and subclass) |
| **Execution Speed** | Faster execution as binding occurs at compile time | Slightly slower due to runtime method lookup |
| **Return Type** | Can have different return types if parameters differ | Must be identical (or covariant return type) |

#### 1. Compile-Time Polymorphism Example (Method Overloading)
```java
class Calculator {
    // Overloaded add methods with different parameter signatures
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}
```

#### 2. Runtime Polymorphism Example (Method Overriding)
```java
class Shape {
    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing generic shape");
    }
}

class Circle extends Shape {
    @Override
    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing a Circle");
    }
}

public class TestPolymorphism {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Shape s = new Circle(); // Upcasting
        s.draw(); // Output: Drawing a Circle (resolved at runtime)
    }
}
```

