solutions.oopj|explain how inheritance works in ja:
### How Inheritance Works in Java

**Inheritance** allows a child class to **reuse** the fields and methods of a parent class. The child class can also **extend** (add new features) and **override** (modify existing behavior).

**Keyword:** `extends`

**Rules:**
- Java supports single inheritance for classes (one parent only)
- Constructor of parent is called using `super()`
- `final` classes cannot be inherited
- `private` members are not inherited (accessible only within parent)

**Complete Example:**
```java
// Parent class
class Shape {
    String color;

    Shape(String color) {
        this.color = color;
    }

    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing a " + color + " shape");
    }

    double area() {
        return 0;
    }
}

// Child class – inherits Shape
class Circle extends Shape {
    double radius;

    Circle(String color, double radius) {
        super(color);          // calls Shape constructor
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    @Override
    double area() {            // overrides Shape's area()
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }

    @Override
    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing " + color + " circle of radius " + radius);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Circle c = new Circle("red", 5.0);
        c.draw();
        System.out.printf("Area: %.2f%n", c.area());

        // Polymorphism – parent reference
        Shape s = new Circle("blue", 3.0);
        s.draw();
        System.out.printf("Area: %.2f%n", s.area());
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```
Drawing red circle of radius 5.0
Area: 78.54
Drawing blue circle of radius 3.0
Area: 28.27
```

