### Nested Class Concept with Example

**Nested class:** A class defined within another class. It has access to the outer class's members.

**Types:**
1. **Static nested class** – Can access only outer class's static members
2. **Non-static inner class** – Can access all outer class members (including private)

**Example:**
```java
public class University {
    private String name = "MGIT";   // private outer class field

    // Static nested class
    static class Department {
        static String dept = "CSE";

        void showDept() {
            System.out.println("Department: " + dept);
            // Cannot access 'name' here (non-static outer field)
        }
    }

    // Non-static inner class – can access 'name'
    class Student {
        String studentName;
        int rollNo;

        Student(String sName, int roll) {
            studentName = sName;
            rollNo = roll;
        }

        void display() {
            // Accessing outer class private field 'name'
            System.out.println("University: " + name);
            System.out.println("Student: " + studentName + ", Roll: " + rollNo);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1. Static nested class – no outer instance needed
        Department.showDept();  // or: new Department().showDept();

        // 2. Inner class – needs outer instance
        University uni = new University();
        University.Student s = uni.new Student("Ravi", 101);
        s.display();
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```
Department: CSE
University: MGIT
Student: Ravi, Roll: 101
```

**When to use nested classes:**
- When a class logically belongs to only one outer class
- To increase encapsulation
- To make code more readable and maintainable

