solutions.oopj|briefly discuss about the control s:
### Control Statements in Java

Control statements direct the flow of execution in a Java program.

**Three categories:**

---

**1. Decision-Making Statements:**

`if`, `if-else`, `if-else if`, `switch`
```java
// if-else
if (age >= 18)
    System.out.println("Adult");
else
    System.out.println("Minor");

// switch
switch (day) {
    case 1: System.out.println("Mon"); break;
    case 2: System.out.println("Tue"); break;
    default: System.out.println("Other");
}
```

---

**2. Loop Statements:**

`for`, `while`, `do-while`, enhanced-for

```java
// for loop
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
    System.out.print(i + " ");  // 1 2 3 4 5

// while loop
int i = 1;
while (i <= 3) { System.out.print(i++ + " "); }  // 1 2 3

// do-while (executes at least once)
int j = 10;
do { System.out.println(j); j++; } while (j < 5);  // prints 10 (once)

// enhanced for
int[] arr = {10, 20, 30};
for (int x : arr) System.out.print(x + " ");  // 10 20 30
```

---

**3. Jump Statements:**

`break`, `continue`, `return`
```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    if (i == 6)  break;      // exits the loop
    if (i % 2 == 0) continue; // skips even numbers
    System.out.print(i + " "); // prints 1 3 5
}
```

