### Nested and Inner Classes in Java

**Nested class:** A class defined inside another class.

**Types:**
1. Static nested class (accessed without outer object)
2. Inner class (non-static – needs outer object)
3. Local class (inside a method)
4. Anonymous class (defined and instantiated in one expression)

```java
public class OuterClass {
    private int outerVar = 10;

    // 1. Static Nested Class
    static class StaticNested {
        void display() {
            System.out.println("Static Nested Class");
            // Cannot access outerVar directly (no outer instance)
        }
    }

    // 2. Inner Class (Non-Static)
    class InnerClass {
        void display() {
            // Can access outer class private members!
            System.out.println("Inner Class – outerVar = " + outerVar);
        }
    }

    void showLocal() {
        // 3. Local Class (inside a method)
        class LocalClass {
            void greet() { System.out.println("Local Class inside method"); }
        }
        new LocalClass().greet();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Access static nested class
        StaticNested sn = new StaticNested();
        sn.display();

        // Access inner class – needs outer class instance
        OuterClass outer = new OuterClass();
        InnerClass inner = outer.new InnerClass();
        inner.display();

        // Local class
        outer.showLocal();

        // 4. Anonymous class (implements Runnable on-the-fly)
        Runnable r = new Runnable() {
            public void run() {
                System.out.println("Anonymous Class running!");
            }
        };
        r.run();
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```
Static Nested Class
Inner Class – outerVar = 10
Local Class inside method
Anonymous Class running!
```

