### Differences Between Class and Abstract Class in Java

| Criteria | Concrete Class | Abstract Class |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Instantiation** | Can be directly instantiated using `new` | Cannot be instantiated directly with `new` |
| **Keyword** | Defined with `class` | Defined with `abstract class` |
| **Method Implementation** | Can only contain fully implemented methods | Can contain both abstract methods (without body) and concrete methods |
| **Purpose** | Provides complete blueprints for creating live objects | Serves as a base template to be extended by subclasses |
| **Multiple Inheritance** | Does not support multiple inheritance | Does not support multiple class inheritance |

#### Code Example
```java
// Abstract Class (Template)
abstract class BankAccount {
    String accountHolder;
    BankAccount(String name) { this.accountHolder = name; }

    abstract double calculateInterest(); // Abstract method (no body)

    void printStatement() { // Concrete method
        System.out.println("Account Statement for: " + accountHolder);
    }
}

// Concrete Class (Full Implementation)
class SavingsAccount extends BankAccount {
    double balance;
    SavingsAccount(String name, double bal) {
        super(name);
        this.balance = bal;
    }

    @Override
    double calculateInterest() {
        return balance * 0.04;
    }
}
```

