### Differences Between Interface and Abstract Class in Java

| Feature | Interface | Abstract Class |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Methods** | Originally only abstract; Java 8+ allows default/static methods | Can have both abstract and fully concrete methods |
| **Variables** | Always `public static final` (constants) | Can have instance, static, final, or non-final variables |
| **Multiple Inheritance**| A class can implement multiple interfaces | A class can extend only one abstract class |
| **Constructor** | Does not have constructors | Can define constructors invoked via `super()` |
| **Keyword** | `interface` and `implements` | `abstract class` and `extends` |

```java
interface Drawable {
    void draw(); // pure abstract
}

abstract class GraphicObject {
    int x, y;
    GraphicObject(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
    abstract void render();
}
```

