solutions.oopj|multiple inheritance can be achieve:
### How Multiple Inheritance is Achieved Using Interfaces

Java does not support multiple inheritance with classes to avoid the **Diamond Problem** (ambiguity when two parents provide conflicting method implementations).

Instead, Java allows a class to **implement multiple interfaces**:
- Interfaces provide abstract method signatures with no state conflicts.
- The implementing class provides the single unified implementation.

```java
interface Printer { void print(); }
interface Scanner { void scan(); }

class AllInOneMachine implements Printer, Scanner {
    public void print() { System.out.println("Printing document"); }
    public void scan() { System.out.println("Scanning document"); }
}
```

