### Multilevel Inheritance in Java

**Concept:**
Multilevel inheritance occurs when a derived class inherits from a base class, and another class subsequently inherits from that derived class, forming an inheritance chain:
$$\text{Class A (Grandparent)} \longleftarrow \text{Class B (Parent)} \longleftarrow \text{Class C (Child)}$$

#### Complete Working Program:
```java
// Level 1: Base Class
class Person {
    String name;
    Person(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
    void showPerson() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
    }
}

// Level 2: Intermediate Derived Class
class Employee extends Person {
    int empId;
    Employee(String name, int empId) {
        super(name); // Call Person constructor
        this.empId = empId;
    }
    void showEmployee() {
        showPerson();
        System.out.println("Employee ID: " + empId);
    }
}

// Level 3: Most Derived Class
class Manager extends Employee {
    String department;
    Manager(String name, int empId, String department) {
        super(name, empId); // Call Employee constructor
        this.department = department;
    }
    void showManager() {
        showEmployee();
        System.out.println("Department: " + department);
    }
}

public class MultilevelDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Manager mgr = new Manager("Alice Johnson", 4012, "Cloud Systems");
        mgr.showManager();
    }
}
```

#### Key Architectural Rules:
- Constructors are invoked in top-down order (Object $\to$ Person $\to$ Employee $\to$ Manager).
- Child class gains access to non-private fields and methods across all ancestor levels.

