oopj|explain how runtime polymorphism is.answerMd:
### Runtime Polymorphism via Dynamic Method Dispatch

**Definition:**
**Dynamic Method Dispatch** is the mechanism by which a call to an overridden method is resolved at **runtime** rather than compile-time. This is the foundation of runtime polymorphism in Java.

#### Core Principle:
When an overridden method is invoked through a superclass reference variable, Java determines which version of that method to execute based on the **actual type of the object being referred to**, not the type of the reference variable.

$$\text{SuperClass ref} = \text{new SubClass()}; \quad \text{ref.display()} \implies \text{SubClass's display() executes}$$

#### Complete Java Program:
```java
// Base Class
class Shape {
    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing a generic shape");
    }
}

// Subclass 1
class Circle extends Shape {
    @Override
    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing a Circle with radius r");
    }
}

// Subclass 2
class Rectangle extends Shape {
    @Override
    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing a Rectangle with length and breadth");
    }
}

public class DynamicDispatchDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Shape ref; // Superclass reference

        ref = new Circle();
        ref.draw(); // Calls Circle's draw() at runtime

        ref = new Rectangle();
        ref.draw(); // Calls Rectangle's draw() at runtime
    }
}
```

#### Virtual Method Table (vtable):
The JVM uses an internal **Virtual Method Table (vtable)** allocated per class to resolve method addresses dynamically based on the instantiated object's heap pointer.

